<Layout title="Write Clear and Concise Website Copy | Holy Webs" description="Begin with the question a customer brings to the page." canonicalUrl="https://holywebs.com/resources/blog/simplify-your-message-writing-clear-and-concise-copy/"><div class="migration-page"><header><nav aria-label="Breadcrumb"><a href="/resources/">Resources</a> / <a href="/resources/blog/">Blog</a></nav><h1>Write Clear and Concise Website Copy</h1></header><article><p>Original article by Travis Whatley. Adapted for the current Holy Webs website.</p><section><h2>Write to one reader</h2><p>Begin with the question a customer brings to the page. Explain the service using words you would use in a helpful conversation. Define technical terms when they help someone make a decision; remove them when they add no meaning.</p></section><section><h2>Give the page one primary job</h2><p>Outline the offer, who it helps, what the customer needs to know and the next step. A page can answer several related questions while keeping one main purpose. Move unrelated detail to a useful linked resource.</p></section><section><h2>Make the structure easy to scan</h2><p>Use descriptive headings and paragraphs that develop one idea. Paragraph length should fit the content, not a rigid sentence count. Lists help with steps or comparable choices. Images should explain something or provide evidence rather than interrupt the reading.</p></section><section><h2>Replace vague promises with specifics</h2><p>Instead of “We deliver innovative solutions,” explain the work: “We design service pages that explain your coverage and make it easy to request a quote.” This is an example of clearer wording, not a promise of a particular result.</p></section><section><h2>Read and test the draft</h2><p>Read the copy aloud, then ask someone unfamiliar with the business what they think it offers. Look for awkward phrasing, missing context and unsupported claims. Shorter is useful only when the reader still has enough information.</p></section><section><h2>Use a final editing checklist</h2><p>Can the reader identify the service, decide whether it fits, understand the evidence and find the next step? Does the button describe what happens next? Check those questions before polishing individual words.</p></section><section><h2>Plan your next step</h2><p>Start with your <a href="/resources/blog/defining-your-websites-purpose-and-target-audience/">website purpose and audience</a>, or <a href="/contact/">contact Holy Webs</a> to discuss your priorities.</p></section></article></div>
